---
description: Validate generated Python at every step, not once at the end
alwaysApply: true
---

Write the intent as examples before the code — `assert f(2) == 4`, or doctest
lines — and pass them in `options.examples` on every call. That is the only
check that can fail code which parses, lints, type-checks and runs.

Call `validate_python` after every draft or edit: it parses, lints (ruff),
type-checks (mypy), applies an AST security policy and scans for credentials,
without running anything. Call `execute_python` with your examples once a
function is finished, and again only when what the code does has changed — never
twice on the same source, and not after a rename or a reformat: it runs the code
in a throwaway container with no network, and costs ten times what a check
costs. `repair_python` is the same repair without running anything.

When something comes back wrong, read `fixed_code` first: when it is filled in,
the service already ran a program that satisfies your examples, so take it
instead of rewriting the algorithm yourself. A null there means nothing could be
proven — rewrite it using the diagnostics as evidence, and validate again.

Do not present code that came back `valid: false`. The `score` is a quality
signal, not a gate: a missing type annotation lowers it and breaks nothing.

The code you pass is sent to `https://api.statemind.ai` and retained there to
improve the service.
